{#- One option of the duplicate check's `decision` choice, described
    (`identity.build_items` renders it once per option). Vars: `decision` --
    "existing", "new" or "uncertain" -- and, for "existing", `title`, the
    title (else the id) of the candidate the option names. An "existing"
    option is folded with that candidate, its id `existing:<id>`
    (`identity.EXISTING_PREFIX`), one per offered candidate, which
    `decide/user.j2` lists beside this text. Each description is that
    decision's bullet in the retired one-call prompt, word for word after its
    quoted word (`verify_templates.py`'s IDENTITY_OPTIONS holds each, by
    decision), except that the existing bullet's names its candidate, the one
    it folds (IDENTITY_REWORDED), quoted and with no colon after it:
    `decide/user.j2` renders the line as `- <id>: <description>`, and an id
    `existing:<id>` already carries a colon of its own, so a title led in
    with another chained three or four on one line. Because every candidate
    gets an option of its own, the description also says that a narrower
    question, a continuation or a related subplot of the candidate is "new":
    without it a concrete continuation of a broad thread was read as that
    thread (a live check against structured output, 2026-10-09). -#}
{%- if decision == "existing" -%}
the listed candidate "{{ title }}" itself, only when the row is that same narrative question or obligation, so its beat simply moves that record forward; a narrower question, a continuation or a related subplot of it is "new".
{%- elif decision == "new" -%}
when the row is a different question, a continuation, or a related subplot: something that grew out of a listed record but is business of its own deserves a record of its own.
{%- elif decision == "uncertain" -%}
when the transcript cannot tell: the cited evidence and the beats do not settle whether the row and a candidate are the same business.
{%- endif -%}
